 the chosen threshold, begin no new restriction or compensation plan, and continue qualified clinical guidance. The reader ends curious rather than cornered, with the prospect that the debate itself—not merely their behavior—can end.\n\n## Final verdict\n\n**Assigned transition verdict:** The chapter successfully moves the reader from expecting another discipline-and-deprivation program to understanding that they may investigate while eating normally and retaining full autonomy. Recognition is strong and personal, especially around recurring negotiations, exceptions, recovery, and starting over. The distinction between a “well-managed truce” and the debate being over provides the central turn, while the repeated assurances against immediate restriction consolidate rather than postpone it. The chapter does not prematurely conduct the benefit-demolition reserved for later chapters; it introduces that inquiry as a testable possibility. It leaves the reader with the assigned destination—freedom “without deprivation, without battle, and without a lifetime of rules”—and with the correct practical handoff into the next chapter.\n\n**Largest momentum loss:** NONE"},"sequence_number":532}

